# UI/UX — accessibility

`context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md` is the reference for any interactive or
visual change and for review. Accessibility is P0: it outranks aesthetics and is
not a later pass. Read it with `context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine),
`context/instructions/ui-components.md` (states and interaction) and
`context/instructions/browser.md` (the render-level audit tooling).

## POUR

Accessible content is **Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust**: usable
without sight, without a pointer, with assistive technology, and legible under
interruption. Most failures are violations of one of the four.

## Name, role, state

- Every interactive control has an accessible **name** (a label, not a
  placeholder), a correct **role**, and exposes its **state** (expanded, selected,
  checked, busy, invalid).
- Icon-only controls need a name; decorative elements are hidden from assistive
  tech rather than labelled.
- Prefer the platform's own element and ARIA pattern over a hand-rolled
  `div` with roles.

## Keyboard and focus

- Everything operable by pointer is operable by keyboard, in a logical order.
- Focus is always visible and never lost; focus order follows reading order.
- Escape closes a transient surface; Tab is never trapped without an exit.

## Contrast and targets

- Text meets the project's minimum contrast in **both** themes; large text has a
  lower floor but still one.
- Interactive targets are large enough to hit (commonly 44×44 touch, 40×40 dense
  desktop — the project's own minimum wins); do not shrink a target below it to
  fit a layout. When the visible control is smaller, keep its visible size and
  grow the hit area with a pseudo-element, and **two hit areas must never
  overlap** — shrink one where they would.
- Never rely on colour alone to convey a state or a change.

## Motion and reduced motion

- `prefers-reduced-motion` must preserve **hierarchy and feedback**, not kill all
  motion with a global near-zero duration. Remove decorative motion; keep the
  transition that explains a change of state.
- One authored moment per surface beats the same entrance on every section.
- Motion conveys state; it is not decoration.

## Internationalisation and direction

- Do not bake text into images or assume a string's length or word order.
- Layouts survive **RTL** mirroring and a 30-40% text expansion from translation.
- Format dates, numbers and currency with the platform's locale facilities, not
  by hand.

## Cognitive

- One primary action per surface; keep the path to the goal short.
- Error and empty states tell the user what happened and what to do next.
- Prefer plain language; expand an acronym on first use.

## Review — heuristics, not a score

- A usability review may walk a small checklist (the ten usability heuristics,
  scored 0-4 with a mode-aware `n/a` and a renormalised total) and report
  findings by severity. This is a **usability instrument, not a taste or
  visual-quality score** — do not reduce design judgement to a number.
- A clean scan is not a pass: it does not replace looking at the render. Declare
  a skipped or degraded check; never let it pass as complete (see
  `context/instructions/ui.md`).

## Boundary

- The render-level audit (contrast sampling, focus rings, overflow) needs a real
  render: `context/instructions/browser.md`.
- Component structure and states: `context/instructions/ui-components.md`.
- Visual direction and copy tone: `context/instructions/ui-taste.md`.
